From: DeltaVector1 <DeltaVector1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 16:52:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] Find why the link cannot carry more: 95% of it is
 expert routing

Measured one /inference/v1/generate response. routed_experts is 95.5% of the
compressed bytes; tokens and logprobs together are under 5%. It is 711 bytes
for each generated token, already uint8 at exactly 1 byte an element, and it
gzips only 1.4x because it is already binary. Shape is [positions, 78 layers,
8 experts], so every token costs 624 expert indices.

That is 2.79 MB for each rollout on the wire and about 5.2 Mbit/s at 14
rollouts a minute, across a path measured at 7.5% packet loss and 210 ms.

So the batch size is not limited by tunnels, concurrency, or the engines. It is
limited by shipping an expert routing table for every token. enable_router_
replay = true is what asks for it, and turning it off would cut the payload
about 20x.

Topology found while chasing this: the engines reach n82 through ProxyJump via
a jump host, so every tunnel is SSH inside SSH and the lossy leg carries two
layers of flow control. The bulk flows H200 -> n82, so the H200 is the sender
and its congestion control is the one that matters -- and BBR cannot be loaded
there because the engine hosts are Docker containers without CAP_SYS_MODULE.
A userspace FEC tunnel would avoid that, but UDP and arbitrary TCP to the jump
host both time out; only SSH on 31331 crosses.

scripts/network/tune_wan.sh applies what is available: BBR on n82 for the two
engine routes only, plus tcp_slow_start_after_idle=0, tcp_mtu_probing=1 and a
32 MB buffer ceiling on both ends. BBR is deliberately not global on n82: NCCL
crosses the same interface, where cubic measured 87% against BBR's 36%.

